Z||000014||Robert||03-30-2000||11:20 PM||||Hi Dave. <BR>At my printing shop we have a policy of not making changes for the exception.  I’ve always thought that was a good idea.  I don’t remember where I came up with that rule, but it has worked well for a long time now. <BR>We do about 450 invoices a month, with about 10 bad checks a year.  Not a bad record, all in all.  It would take much more than a few bad checks to get me to change my policy.  <BR>I turn over the bad checks to a collection agency and they get about half the people to pay up, but they take 40%.<BR>I’ve enjoyed dealing with you in the past, and hope to do some deals in the future.<P>Robert<BR>Yellow 87GT<BR> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||olympic||03-31-2000||03:10 AM||||   Alot of people (who have never run a businesss) think that because you are a business owner you're automatically a multi millionaire who won't miss a few dollars here and there when they don't pay up.  The truth is, many business owners, myself included, work 12 hour days 7 days a week for less than minimum wage.  We do it because we love running a business and providing a top quality product or service.<P>     Even though I run my taxi business in a small town, I've had my share of bad debts.  But as others in this post have said, it hasn't changed my policy.  I've never asked anyone to even sign a bill for their charges. It's on their word.  But if they rip me off once, they will be walking for a long time.  Not alot of fun in a Saskatchewan winter.||||reg||1
